Ladybird sizes a popup by measuring its document at 25×25 and taking
the root’s scroll size, up to 800×600, again after each layout that
changed something. Blink measures the same way when its feature
AutoSizeUsesScrollWidthForOverflow is on: FrameViewAutoSizeInfo::
AutoSizeIfNeeded() resets the frame to its minimum size and reads the
scroll width, at the first autosize, after loading, and on layout or
overflow changes, within the bounds Chromium’s ExtensionPopup sets.

A popup closes itself with window.close() whatever its history holds.
Chromium does that for all of an extension’s pages, since
extension_webkit_preferences.cc sets allow_scripts_to_close_windows for
them, and ExtensionHost::CloseContents() then closes the host; Ladybird
does it for an extension’s popups, and not yet for its other pages.
